{
  "0": "Across all four datasets, replace absolute padded-slot position IDs with cumulative nonpadding position IDs, assigning observed items positions 1 through history length and padding position 0. This may improve alignment between training prefixes and inference histories by removing position shifts caused by left padding. Retain truncation, recent-four-state pooling, final-real-position readout, features, embedding parameterization, causal backbone, scoring, loss and optimizer. Synchronize predictor metadata and writer.",
  "1": "Across all four datasets, increase embedding and matching hidden-channel width from 64 to 96 while retaining tied input/output item embeddings. Greater representation capacity may distinguish item interests without the regression previously observed from untying embeddings. Preserve feature definitions, six-block causal topology, dilations, history policy, pooling, dot-product scoring, loss and optimizer. Save the width in metadata and reconstruct the identical single model in prediction.",
  "2": "Across all four datasets, change only the six causal blocks' dilation schedule from [1,2,4,8,1,2] to [1,2,4,1,2,4]. This schedule improved validation on an earlier input program; test whether its emphasis on nearby interactions also helps the current frequency-attenuated incumbent. Preserve block count, convolutions, features, embeddings, readout, scoring, loss and optimizer. Update predictor reconstruction consistently; retain one checkpoint per category.",
  "3": "Across all four datasets, change the training-count feature from sqrt(z) to z**0.25, where z=log1p(count)/max(log1p(count)); retain attenuation 1-0.3*feature and feature zero for unseen items. Stronger attenuation of moderately frequent historical items may extend the incumbent's frequency-transform gain. Compute counts solely from training interactions. Preserve learned embedding design, backbone, readout, scoring, loss and optimizer, and store the feature buffer in each category's single checkpoint."
}
